content.text:
Since the data changes "a few times a week" but is read constantly, you are in the ideal territory for caching. However, "forever" is a dangerous word in systems design.

If you cache "forever," you've essentially turned your database into a static file. The moment the library decides to close for a public holiday, your users will see the wrong hours until you manually restart the server or clear the cache.

To help you choose the right option, we need to look at how the system handles the "stale" window—the gap between when the data changes in the DB and when the user sees it.

```keating-ui
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "caching-options-analysis",
  "revision": 0,
  "lifecycle": "ready",
  "retention": "ephemeral",
  "supportedSurfaces": ["web", "desktop", "mobile", "terminal"],
  "nodes": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "intro",
      "content": "### The Trade-off: Freshness vs. Performance\n\nCaching is the art of deciding how long you are willing to be wrong. For a library page, being wrong for 5 minutes is usually fine; being wrong for a week is a disaster."
    },
    {
      "type": "question",
      "id": "scenario-check",
      "prompt": "If the library closes unexpectedly for an emergency, how quickly MUST that change be reflected on the page for the user?",
      "kind": "choice",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "instant",
          "label": "Instantly (Seconds)"
        },
        {
          "id": "relaxed",
          "label": "Eventually (Minutes to an hour)"
        },
        {
          "id": "very-relaxed",
          "label": "I can manually clear the cache when it changes"
        }
      ],
      "allowText": true
    }
  ],
  "createdAt": "2026-09-07T12:10:00.000Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-09-07T12:10:00.000Z"
}
```

