content.text:
I would love to. The only way to move this from "I think I get it" to "I own this" is to apply it to a new scenario.

Let's try a scenario where the array is slightly different. Imagine you are building a leaderboard for a game.

```javascript
const leaderboard = ["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie", "Diana", "Elena"];
// The length here is 5.
```

I'm going to give you a piece of code, and I want you to **predict the output** without running it first. 

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < leaderboard.length; i++) {
    console.log(leaderboard[i]);
}
```

```keating-ui
{"schemaVersion":1,"id":"leaderboard-challenge","revision":0,"lifecycle":"ready","retention":"ephemeral","supportedSurfaces":["web","desktop","mobile","terminal"],"nodes":[{"type":"markdown","id":"task","content":"Look at the loop above carefully. Notice the `<` symbol (strictly less than)."},{"type":"question","id":"predict-output","prompt":"What will the console print?","kind":"choice","choices":[{"id":"five-names","label":"It will print all 5 names perfectly."},{"id":"four-names","label":"It will print the first 4 names and stop."},{"id":"undefined","label":"It will print 5 names and then 'undefined' at the end."}],"allowText":true}],"createdAt":"2026-09-07T12:35:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-07T12:35:00.000Z"}
```

When we talk about an "average" (specifically the *arithmetic mean*), we are looking for a single number that represents the "center" of a set of data. Imagine you have several piles of coins of different heights; if you pushed all the coins together into one big heap and then redistributed them perfectly evenly across the original number of piles, the height of each pile would be the average. It's the balance point where the total sum of the data is shared equally.

To keep the math clear, statisticians use specific symbols so they don't have to write "the average of the group" every time. In a formula, you'll often see $\bar{x}$ (pronounced "x-bar"), which is just a shorthand way of saying "**the average of the values we measured**." You'll also see a large $\Sigma$ (the Greek letter Sigma), which is a command that means "**add everything up**." So, when you see $\Sigma x$, it's simply telling you to take every single individual value ($x$) in your list and find their total sum.

