content.text:
That "extra undefined" is a classic rite of passage in programming. It happens because you are asking JavaScript for a value at a position that doesn't actually exist.

If `names.length` is 3, the valid indexes are `0`, `1`, and `2`. 
When your loop hits `i = 3` (because you used `<=`), you are asking for `names[3]`. Since there is nothing there, JavaScript gives you `undefined`.

Let's visualize exactly what's happening. Look at this trace:

```javascript
const names = ["Ada", "Bo", "Cy"];
// names.length is 3

// Loop 1: i = 0 -> names[0] is "Ada" ✅
// Loop 2: i = 1 -> names[1] is "Bo"  ✅
// Loop 3: i = 2 -> names[2] is "Cy"  ✅
// Loop 4: i = 3 -> names[3] is ... ?  ❌ (undefined!)
```

To fix this, you have to decide: do you want the loop to stop *before* it hits the length, or do you want to change how you calculate the index?

Predict what happens in these two scenarios:

```keating-ui
{"schemaVersion":1,"id":"index-logic-fix","revision":0,"lifecycle":"ready","retention":"ephemeral","supportedSurfaces":["web","desktop","mobile","terminal"],"nodes":[{"type":"question-group","id":"comparison-group","nodes":[{"type":"question","id":"strict-less","prompt":"If you change `i <= names.length` to `i < names.length`, will the loop still reach 'Cy'?","kind":"choice","choices":[{"id":"yes","label":"Yes, it will stop at index 2"},{"id":"no","label":"No, it will stop at index 1"}],"allowText":true},{"type":"question","id":"length-minus-one","prompt":"If you kept `i <=`, what would you have to change `names.length` to so that the loop stops perfectly at the last element?","kind":"choice","choices":[{"id":"plus-one","label":"names.length + 1"},{"id":"minus-one","label":"names.length - 1"},{"id":"zero","label":"0"}],"allowText":true}]}],"createdAt":"2026-09-07T12:14:21.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-07T12:14:21.000Z"}
```

You've hit on one of the most fundamental "aha!" moments in programming. The idea that a different name should mean a different thing is intuitive—in English, if I give a "house" a second name like "the home," it's still just one building. 

But in JavaScript, the way variables hold arrays is a bit more subtle.

To see exactly what happened to `a`, let's run your code and look at the result.

```javascript
const a = [1, 2]; 
const b = a; 
b.push(3);

console.log("a:", a); 
console.log("b:", b);
```

If you run this, you'll see that `a` is actually `[1, 2, 3]`.

Since you predicted `a` would remain `[1, 2]`, we need to uncover why the "different name" didn't create a "different list."

```keating-ui
{"schemaVersion":1,"id":"reference-intuition","revision":0,"lifecycle":"ready","retention":"ephemeral","supportedSurfaces":["web","desktop","mobile","terminal"],"nodes":[{"type":"question","id":"pointer-logic","prompt":"If `a` and `b` are both pointing to the same 'bucket' of data in memory, what happens to the data in that bucket when you use the name `b` to add something to it?","kind":"choice","choices":[{"id":"isolated","label":"The change only affects the name `b`"},{"id":"shared","label":"The change affects the bucket itself, and anyone looking at that bucket sees the change"}],"allowText":true,"hint":"Think about the 'bucket' (the array) as being separate from the 'names' (the variables)."]}],"createdAt":"2026-09-07T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-07T00:00:00.000Z"}
```

